    "final_text": "I've built the CSV module, and all 61 tests pass under `npm test` (Node 24). Nothing is committed.\n\n**Files**\n- `src/csv.js` exports `parse`, `createParser`, `stringify` and `CsvError`, with no dependencies.\n- `package.json` runs `node --test test/*.test.js`.\n- `test/parse.test.js`, `test/stream.test.js` and `test/stringify.test.js` hold the tests. `test/helpers.js` has shared helpers and a second, deliberately simple parser that the tests check against.\n\n**Design:** `parse` and `createParser` run the same code, so they can't disagree. It reads each character once and carries anything unfinished between chunks: a partial field, a CR whose LF may come next, or a `\"` that might be the first half of `\"\"`. It never re-reads earlier input.\n\n**How it's tested**\n- **Fixed cases:** every rule in the spec, including the exact line and column of each error and which error wins when there are several.\n- **Chunking:** every way of splitting each sample input into three chunks, plus one character at a time, must give the same records or the same error as `parse`.\n- **Random input:** 25,000 random inputs are checked, against the second parser and with random chunking.\n- **Round trip:** 20,000 random `stringify` \u2192 `parse` checks, across delimiters, both `eol` values, and with and without `skipEmptyLines`.\n- **Speed:** 1 MB inputs fed one character at a time must finish within a time limit.\n\n**Speed:** `parse` handles a 16 MB file with a header in about 160 ms. Streaming it in 7-character chunks takes about 180 ms.\n\n**Choices where the spec was ambiguous**\n- **BOM and columns:** a leading BOM doesn't count as a column, so column 1 is the first character after it.\n- **Duplicate header names** are detected when the repeated field ends. So in `a,\"a\"x`, the bad `x` is reported first, but in `a,a,\"b` the duplicate is reported before the unclosed quote.\n- **Wrong field count:** the error gives the line where the record starts, which matters when a quoted field spans lines.\n- **CR at the end of a chunk:** the record is returned by that `write`, without waiting to see whether an LF follows.\n- **Any throw ends the parser,** including a `TypeError` from `write` given a non-string.\n- **\"Single character\" means one UTF-16 code unit,** so an emoji delimiter throws `TypeError`.\n- **`stringify` with a header:** the header row is still written when `rows` is empty (`'a,b\\r\\n'`); I read \"no rows gives `''`\" as applying only without a header. `header` must be an array of strings, `header: true` throws `TypeError`, and object rows only use their own properties.\n\n**One addition to the quoting rules:** `parse` drops a leading BOM, so `stringify` also quotes the first field when the output would otherwise start with U+FEFF. Without this, a row like `['\\uFEFFa']` wouldn't survive the round trip that the spec requires.",
